THE SCAFFOLD COUNT'S POPULATION, GENERATED AT RUN TIME AND NEVER READ FROM A FILE.

This is the structural half of incant/fixits/countInputInTmp. The harness used to
read its rule list from /tmp/b2/rules.txt; when that vanished on 2026-08-24 the
list was rebuilt by scraping rule names out of the PREVIOUS RUN'S OWN LOG, which
is circular -- it can only ever return what the last run happened to attempt.
Measured 2026-08-28: the resulting 42-name file was byte-identical to the names
in /tmp/b2/count.out, and it had already drifted -- it carried `CodE`, a rule
renamed out of existence, and lacked `CodeBody`, the rule that replaced it. The
ghost still scored `ok`.

Deriving the list here makes that unconstructable rather than unlikely. There is
no file to go stale, because the list IS the population.

THE FILTERS ARE THE ODOMETER'S FOUR PLUS ONE, and the fifth is Tony's ruling of
2026-08-28: a rule must HAVE A LIST to be worth compiling a parse body for.
`tokenize` is a method hook with no terms (GroupMain: `method = tokenize`, then
`modify(strap,"^@")`), and break/continue/return are members of the `BrancheS`
bin. All four carry isRule only because modify() calls setRuleStuff(), which
raises the flag -- the same back-propagation named in
incant/fixits/literalMasterIsRule. None of them has a body to generate, so
compiling one asserts nothing.

⚠ THE LENGTH IS CAPTURED TO A LOCAL BEFORE IT IS TESTED, AND THAT IS NOT STYLE.
A property read INSIDE a compound condition is unreliable -- bare or dotted --
and it fails by UNDER-FILTERING at exit 0, which looks exactly like a walk that
is working. Measured six ways, 2026-08-28, one run each, same file, same binary
(39 is correct):

    if listLengtH;                        39   correct   (bare, POSITIVE)
    if !listLengtH;                       64   WRONG
    if listLengtH == 0;                   64   WRONG
    if cpCur.listLengtH == 0;             64   WRONG
    cpLen = listLengtH; if cpLen == 0;    39   correct
    cpLen = listLengtH; if !cpLen;        39   correct

⚠ SO `!` IS NOT THE CULPRIT, WHICH IS THE HALF THAT WOULD HAVE BEEN GOT WRONG.
`== 0` fails identically, and once the value is in a local BOTH `!` and `== 0`
are fine. The broken thing is reading the property in the condition; the
negation is innocent. In every failing arm the condition read as always-truthy,
so nothing was filtered and all 64 came through.

This is bear-trap 35 -- probes read through bare locals -- extending from print
position into condition position. The safe shape is: capture, then test.
⚠ `cpLen` IS DELIBERATELY UNDECLARED -- bear-trap 39: one action touches it, so
it must be an action local; declaring it would make it persist across calls.

⚠ THE CURSOR `cpCur` IS DELIBERATELY NOT DECLARED IN THE define BLOCK. Bear-trap
38: a cursor named there limits the walk to exactly one member, silently, at
exit 0. Do not "tidy" this by declaring it.
